problems.problem:
Suppose that $X+Y+Z$ and $X^2+Y^2+Z^2$ are integers divisible by $6$. Prove that:

(a) if $X,Y,Z$ are integers, then $X^n+Y^n+Z^n$ is an integer divisible by $6$ for every positive integer $n$;

(b) if $X,Y,Z$ are real numbers and $XYZ$ is an even integer, then $X^3+Y^3+Z^3$ is an integer divisible by $6$;

(c) if $X,Y,Z$ are real numbers and $XYZ$ is an even integer, then $X^n+Y^n+Z^n$ is an integer divisible by $6$ for every positive integer $n$.

The annual banquet of the Breton Union of Cold-Water Mussel Fishermen brought together $2020$ participants this year. The cake served as dessert was shared out and distributed (in decreasing order of age) as follows:

— the $1$st received $1/2020$ of the cake;

— the $2$nd received $2/2020$ of the rest;

— the $3$rd received $3/2020$ of the rest;

— $\ldots$

— the $2019$th received $2019/2020$ of the rest;

— the $2020$th received all the rest.

Who received the largest share?
